  "question": {
    "text": "Let $\\alpha, \\beta$ be real and $z$ be a complex number. If $z^2+\\alpha z+\\beta=0$ has two distinct roots on the line $\\operatorname{Re} z=1$, then it is necessary that",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "jZywp",
      "text": "\n$\\beta \\in(-1,0)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "W9ggS",
      "text": "\n$|\\beta|=1$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "uCKq6",
      "text": "\n$\\beta \\in(1, \\infty)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "7p9f5",
      "text": "\n$\\beta \\in(0,1)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Suppose roots are $1+\\mathrm{pi}, 1+\\mathrm{qi}$\nSum of roots $1+p i+1+q i=-\\alpha$ which is real $\\Rightarrow$ roots of $1+\\mathrm{pi}, 1-\\mathrm{pi}$\nProduct of roots $=\\beta=1+p^2 \\in(1, \\infty)$\n$\\mathrm{p} \\neq 0$ since roots are distinct.",
    "image": null
  },
